cture, I need to define a theorem with a specific name and formal statement. The theorem must take points and lines as arguments, forming a triangle, and then must show that if the condition about the squares holds, then the angle is a right angle.\n\nLet me work on the formalization step by step.\n\nFirst, the theorem is about a triangle ABC. So I need points A, B, and C, and the lines AB, BC, and AC forming the triangle.\n\nThen, the theorem states that if the square on side BC is equal to the sum of the squares on sides BA and AC, then angle BAC is a right angle.\n\nIn Lean, the theorem will need to take these points and lines as arguments.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n```\n\nNow, I need to express the condition about the squares and the conclusion about the angle.\n\nThe condition is |b-c|² = |a-b|² + |a-c|². The conclusion is angle a:b:c = 90°.\n\nI need to match the syntax exactly. The conclusion angle is written as ∠ a:b:c, and 90 degrees is written as ∟.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n(h : |(b─c)| * |(b─c)| = |(a─b)| * |(a─b)| + |(a─c)| * |(a─c)|) : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = ∟ := by \n```\n\nNow, I need to make sure the conclusion angle is exactly what's specified. The angle at point b between sides a-b and c-b.\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n(h : |(b─c)| * |(b─c)| = |(a─b)| * |(a─b)| + |(a─c)| * |(a─c)|) : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = ∟ := by\n```\n\nThis looks complete. The theorem takes a triangle formed by points a, b, c and lines AB, BC, AC. The hypothesis h states the condition about the squares, and the goal is to prove the angle is right.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c : Point) (AB BC AC : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n(h : |(b─c)| * |(b─c)| = |(a─b)| * |(a─b)| + |(a─c)| * |(a─c)|) : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = ∟ := by\n  sorry\n```